    <h1 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl lg:text-[28px] font-semibold text-ink tracking-tight mt-0 mb-4 leading-tight">
        Where Do Screenshots Go on Mac? How to Find Them and Change the Folder
    </h1>

    <AuthorByline publishDate="2026-10-01" updatedDate="2026-10-01" />

    <div class="text-body text-base sm:text-[17px] leading-relaxed space-y-4 [&_h2]:text-ink [&_h2]:text-lg [&_h2]:sm:text-xl [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:tracking-tight [&_h2]:mt-8 [&_h2]:mb-2 [&_h2]:leading-tight [&_h3]:text-ink [&_h3]:text-base [&_h3]:sm:text-lg [&_h3]:font-semibold [&_h3]:tracking-tight [&_h3]:mt-6 [&_h3]:mb-2 [&_h3]:leading-tight [&_a]:text-link [&_a]:underline [&_a]:underline-offset-2 [&_a]:decoration-link/30 [&_a]:transition-colors [&_a:hover]:decoration-link">

        <p>A Mac screenshot lands on your Desktop as a PNG file, unless something changed that. People search for this answer because the screenshot is sometimes not there: the Desktop looks empty, the file was filed into a pile, or the capture never became a file at all. This guide covers the default, every way a screenshot ends up somewhere else, and how to pick a folder that suits you.</p>

        <h2>The Default: Desktop, as a PNG</h2>
        <p>On any recent version of macOS, <strong>Cmd+Shift+3</strong> (whole screen) and <strong>Cmd+Shift+4</strong> (a selection) save a file to the Desktop. The name follows the pattern <code>Screenshot [date] at [time].png</code>. Older macOS releases used "Screen Shot" instead of "Screenshot", so a search for the old wording can still turn up files from years ago. The format is PNG, which is why a full-screen capture on a Retina display can be several megabytes. If you need something smaller, see <a href="/blog/convert-png-to-jpg-mac/">how to convert a Mac screenshot from PNG to JPG</a>.</p>

        <figure class="my-6 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft">
            <img src="/blog/where-do-screenshots-go-on-mac.webp" alt="Illustration of the Cmd+Shift+4 shortcut pointing to the Desktop folder and a screenshot file name" width="1600" height="900" class="w-full aspect-[16/9] object-cover" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </figure>

        <VideoLink
            videoId="ffDhy9IjOq8"
            title="Mac: Where do Screenshots Go in macOS"
            note="A short walkthrough of the default screenshot location on a Mac and what to check when a capture is not on the Desktop."
        />

        <h2>Seven Reasons a Screenshot Is Not on Your Desktop</h2>

        <h3>The Save to location was changed</h3>
        <p>Press <strong>Cmd+Shift+5</strong> to open the capture toolbar, then click <strong>Options</strong>. The <strong>Save to</strong> section lists Desktop, Documents, Clipboard, Mail, Messages, Preview, and any folders you used recently, plus <strong>Other Location</strong>. A checkmark shows the current choice. macOS keeps that setting until you change it again, so a one-time switch (for example to Documents) quietly applies to every screenshot after it.</p>

        <h3>It went to the clipboard, not a file</h3>
        <p>Adding <strong>Control</strong> to the shortcut, as in <strong>Cmd+Ctrl+Shift+3</strong> or <strong>Cmd+Ctrl+Shift+4</strong>, copies the capture to the clipboard and creates no file. Choosing <strong>Clipboard</strong> under Save to does the same for every capture. In both cases the image exists only in memory until you paste it somewhere, and copying anything else replaces it. This is the most common reason a screenshot seems to vanish. See <a href="#clipboard">how to save a clipboard-only screenshot</a> below.</p>

        <h3>You left the floating thumbnail alone, or dismissed it</h3>
        <p>After a capture, a small thumbnail appears in the bottom-right corner. If you ignore it, the file is saved to your chosen location after a few seconds. Swiping it to the right saves it immediately. Control-clicking it offers options such as opening the screenshot in Markup or moving it to another folder. If you do not like the delay, turn <strong>Show Floating Thumbnail</strong> off in the same Options menu.</p>

        <h3>Desktop Stacks grouped it</h3>
        <p>If you turned on <strong>Use Stacks</strong> (right-click the Desktop, or use the View menu), macOS gathers files into piles by kind, and screenshots get their own pile. The files are there, just stacked in one corner. Click the stack to expand it, or turn Stacks off.</p>

        <h3>The file is on the Desktop, but you are looking at the wrong Desktop</h3>
        <p>If iCloud Drive's Desktop &amp; Documents sync is on, your Desktop is an iCloud folder, and a second Mac signed into the same account shows the same files. If you are on a Mac that has not finished syncing, the file can take a moment to appear.</p>

        <h3>Search for it by what it is</h3>
        <p>macOS tags screenshots when it saves them, so you can find them wherever they went. Open Spotlight with <strong>Cmd+Space</strong> and search for <code>kMDItemIsScreenCapture:1</code>. The same query works in a Finder search field. Sort the results by Date Created to see the newest first.</p>

        <h3>Ask macOS directly in Terminal</h3>
        <p>If you want to confirm the folder, open Terminal and run:</p>
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft p-4 text-sm"><code>defaults read com.apple.screencapture location</code></pre>
        <p>It prints the folder if one has been set. If it reports that the pair does not exist, no custom location is set and the Desktop is in use.</p>

        <h2>How to Change the Screenshot Folder on Mac</h2>
        <p>A dedicated folder keeps your Desktop clean and makes screenshots easy to find. There are two ways to set one.</p>

        <h3>With the Screenshot toolbar</h3>
        <ol class="pl-5 space-y-2 list-decimal">
            <li>Press <strong>Cmd+Shift+5</strong>.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Options</strong>.</li>
            <li>Under <strong>Save to</strong>, pick a folder, or choose <strong>Other Location</strong> and select or create one, such as <code>Pictures/Screenshots</code>.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>With Terminal</h3>
        <p>Create the folder first, then point macOS at it and restart the service that handles captures:</p>
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft p-4 text-sm"><code>mkdir -p ~/Pictures/Screenshots
defaults write com.apple.screencapture location ~/Pictures/Screenshots
killall SystemUIServer</code></pre>
        <p>To go back to the default, run <code>defaults delete com.apple.screencapture location</code> and then <code>killall SystemUIServer</code> again. Screenshots return to the Desktop.</p>

        <VideoLink
            videoId="F1w168N0uFA"
            title="How to Change Where Screenshots Are Saved on macOS"
            note="A visual guide to the Cmd+Shift+5 Options menu and the Save to setting covered in this section."
        />

        <h2 id="clipboard">When the Screenshot Only Exists on the Clipboard</h2>
        <p>If your capture went to the clipboard, you have not lost it, as long as you have not copied anything else. Paste it into <a href="https://pastedownloadapp.com/">PasteDownloadApp</a> with <strong>Cmd+V</strong>. The tool reads the image from your clipboard in the browser, shows a preview, and lets you download it as a PNG, JPEG, or WebP file. Nothing is uploaded. Below is the tool with a test screenshot pasted in.</p>

        <figure class="my-6 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft">
            <img src="/blog/screenshots/paste-tool-mac-test-png-2mb.webp" alt="Screenshot of PasteDownloadApp with a pasted 1280 by 800 test screenshot, showing the PNG output at 2.0 MB" width="576" height="566" class="w-full max-w-lg mx-auto" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </figure>

        <p>This also works for images you copied from a web page or from Preview with <strong>Cmd+C</strong>, which behave like clipboard screenshots. For a longer walkthrough, see <a href="/blog/download-copied-image-mac/">how to download a copied image on Mac</a>. For the same question on other devices, see <a href="/blog/where-do-clipboard-images-go/">where screenshots go on Mac, Windows, Android, and iPhone</a>.</p>

        <h2>A Setup That Avoids the Problem</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5">
            <li>Pick one folder under Save to and keep it, so you always know where files go.</li>
            <li>Learn the one shortcut you use. Without Control, you get a file. With Control, you get a clipboard image.</li>
            <li>Use the Spotlight query above whenever a file seems missing. It finds screenshots in any folder.</li>
        </ul>


        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>Where do screenshots go on Mac by default?</h3>
        <p>They are saved to the Desktop as PNG files named like <code>Screenshot 2026-10-01 at 9.41.07 AM.png</code>. That holds until the save location is changed in the Cmd+Shift+5 Options menu or with a Terminal command.</p>

        <h3>Why are my Mac screenshots not showing up on the Desktop?</h3>
        <p>The most common causes are a changed Save to location, a Control-modified shortcut that copied the image to the clipboard instead of saving a file, or the Desktop grouping files into Stacks. Check <strong>Cmd+Shift+5 &rarr; Options</strong> first, then search Spotlight for <code>kMDItemIsScreenCapture:1</code>.</p>

        <h3>How do I change where screenshots are saved on Mac?</h3>
        <p>Press <strong>Cmd+Shift+5</strong>, click <strong>Options</strong>, and choose a folder under <strong>Save to</strong>. To use a folder that is not in the list, choose <strong>Other Location</strong>. You can also set it in Terminal with <code>defaults write com.apple.screencapture location</code> followed by the folder path, then run <code>killall SystemUIServer</code>.</p>

        <h3>Where do Mac screen recordings go?</h3>
        <p>Screen recordings made from the <strong>Cmd+Shift+5</strong> toolbar use the same Save to location as screenshots, so they land on the Desktop unless you changed it.</p>

